import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
r=Path(r'C:\Users\Mohamed\Desktop\Work\QCopter-Stepwise');teacher=Path(r'C:\Users\Mohamed\Desktop\Work\IntelQCopter-USTHB')
out=r/'docs/learning/quaternion-fusion';out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
raw=r/'artifacts/attitude-preview/20261031T001448/serial-original.log';ls=raw.read_text(errors='replace').splitlines()
q=np.array([[float(v) for v in l.split(',')[1:]] for l in ls if l.startswith('Q,')]);d=np.array([[float(v) for v in l.split(',')[1:]] for l in ls if l.startswith('D,') and len(l.split(','))==13])
norm=np.linalg.norm(q[:,1:],axis=1);unit=q[:,1:]/norm[:,None];delta=np.rad2deg(2*np.arccos(np.clip(np.abs((unit[1:]*unit[:-1]).sum(axis=1)),0,1)))
fig,axs=plt.subplots(3,1,figsize=(11,8),layout='constrained')
t=(q[:,0]-q[0,0])/1000
for i,name in enumerate('wxyz'):axs[0].plot(t,q[:,i+1],label=name,lw=1)
axs[0].legend(ncol=4);axs[0].set_ylabel('Quaternion components')
axs[1].plot(t,(norm-1)*1e6,color='#08794e');axs[1].set_ylabel('(norm(q) - 1) × 10⁶')
axs[2].plot(t[1:],delta,color='#ba6121');axs[2].set(ylabel='Relative rotation (deg)',xlabel='Nominal sample-sequence time (s)')
for ax in axs:ax.grid(alpha=.2)
fig.suptitle('Actual ESP32-S3 quaternion diagnostics | operator deliberately shook assembly\nNumerical behavior only: no external true-orientation reference')
fig.savefig(out/'quaternion-evidence.png',dpi=150);plt.close(fig)
stats={'engine':'Offline NumPy/Matplotlib analysis of actual FUSION_PREVIEW_V2 diagnostics','source_identity':[l for l in ls if l.startswith('HELLO,')],'log_sha256':hashlib.sha256(raw.read_bytes()).hexdigest(),'quaternion_rows':len(q),'norm_min':float(norm.min()),'norm_max':float(norm.max()),'max_relative_step_deg':float(delta.max()),'logged_errors':[l for l in ls if l.startswith('ERROR,')],'operator_reference':'User says result very good and jitter from deliberate shaking','limitation':'No external reference, no physical accuracy/sign or real-time certification; nominal10Hz diagnostics, not raw1kHz history. This run precedes Xbox integration.'}
(out/'evidence.json').write_text(json.dumps(stats,indent=2)+'\n')
css='body{background:#f1f6f3;color:#173d2c;font:18px/1.65 system-ui;margin:0}main{max-width:1000px;margin:auto;padding:35px 22px}header{background:#124b34;color:white;border-radius:18px;padding:30px}img{width:100%;border-radius:12px}h2{margin-top:2em}.eq,details{padding:20px;background:white;border-radius:12px;margin:15px 0}.eq{text-align:center;font:22px/1.8 Georgia}pre{background:#102b20;color:#daf5e4;padding:18px;overflow:auto;font-size:14px}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:15px}td,th{border-bottom:1px solid #cbdfd1;padding:12px;vertical-align:top;text-align:left}.flow{display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap}.flow span{padding:15px;background:#dceee2;border-radius:10px}a{color:#087e4c}summary{cursor:pointer}'
core='''<header><p>IntelQCopter · Important engineering step · 31 October 2026</p><h1>A continuous attitude state</h1><p>From gyro rates and gravity to a normalized quaternion.</p></header>
<h2>1 · What improved</h2><p>The operator reports a good preview response and confirms that visible jitter occurred during deliberate shaking. The recording contains ROWS quaternion snapshots, norm between NMIN and NMAX, and no logged acquisition errors. These support numerical-state health during this run, not exact physical attitude accuracy.</p><img src="quaternion-evidence.png" alt="Actual quaternion components, norm deviation and relative rotation">
<h2>2 · Estimator architecture</h2><div class="flow"><span>MPU6050 → fixed calibration</span><span>Gyro → body rates → quaternion prediction</span><span>Acceleration → gravity correction</span><span>Normalize q → attitude state</span></div><p>Mahony complementary fusion from Adafruit AHRS2.4.0 is used for this preview. This is not a Kalman filter and is not a flight PID. The application remains one Arduino main.cpp. No motor output or flight acceptance.</p>
<h2>3 · Meaning of the quaternion</h2><div class="eq">q = (w, x, y, z) = (cos(α/2), u sin(α/2))<br>u = (uₓ, uᵧ, u_z), ‖u‖ = 1<br>‖q‖² = cos²(α/2) + ‖u‖² sin²(α/2) = 1</div><p>q encodes orientation; w is its scalar part and (x,y,z) its vector part. α is a rotation angle, u its unit axis, and ‖·‖ the Euclidean norm (square root of the sum of squared components). Four numbers with one unit-length constraint represent three local rotational degrees of freedom. q and −q represent the same orientation.</p>
<h2>4 · Prediction and correction</h2><div class="eq">â = a / ‖a‖, &nbsp; e = â × ĝ(q)<br>ω_c = ω + Kₚ e<br>q̇ = ½ q ⊗ (0, ω_c)<br>q* = q + Δt q̇, &nbsp; q_next = q* / ‖q*‖</div><p>a is calibrated body acceleration (m/s²), â its unit direction, ĝ the estimated unit gravity direction in body coordinates. × denotes vector cross product; e is their direction error. ω is bias-corrected body gyro rate in rad/s; ω_c adds proportional gravity feedback. Kₚ=0.5 is the provisional estimator correction gain, not a motor-controller gain. Integral correction is disabled (Kᵢ=0) because the measured gyro bias remains the baseline. ⊗ denotes quaternion multiplication; q̇ is the time derivative; Δt=0.001s is the nominal FIFO measurement interval, not the interval between serial reads. * labels an intermediate value, not a multiplication here. The shown equations explain the pinned library's IMU update and the application's explicit renormalization.</p>
<div class="eq">ĝ(q) = (2(xz−wy), 2(wx+yz), 1−2(x²+y²))</div><p>This body gravity convention matches the preview's positive-Z resting reference. Accelerometer feedback is skipped when |‖a‖−g|≥1.5m/s², with g=9.80665m/s². The threshold is heuristic: some translations keep ‖a‖ close to g and pass this check. Startup requires usable gravity. Heading is initialized to zero and remains a relative reference; no absolute yaw correction is available from accelerometer alone.</p>
<h2>5 · Why four smooth numbers can still display a jump</h2><div class="eq">Δα = 2 acos(|q₁ · q₂|)<br>φ = atan2(2(wx+yz), 1−2(x²+y²))<br>θ = asin(clamp(2(wy−zx), −1, 1))</div><p>For unit quaternions q₁,q₂, their dot product · is the sum of pairwise component products; absolute value handles the equivalent signs q and−q. acos converts the relative rotation to an angle. This avoids confusing an Euler wrap with physical rotation. φ is roll and θ pitch; these displayed coordinates remain ambiguous near vertical pitch and roll wraps at±180degrees. clamp bounds asin's input against rounding errors; it does not eliminate the coordinate singularity. Future feedback must use a well-defined orientation error or an explicitly bounded operating region.</p>
<h2>6 · What this run does and does not validate</h2><p>The state remained normalized during the operator's movement. Relative rotation is computed from each pair of normalized diagnostic quaternions; the plot is not a ground-truth motion trace. Maximum diagnostic step was STEPdegrees; deliberate shaking can produce substantial movement, but no external reference establishes the true angle or exact causes of every change. Source and raw-log hashes are in <a href="evidence.json">evidence.json</a>. Existing calibration was not refitted.</p>
<h2>7 · Next: commands before motors</h2><div class="flow"><span>Xbox stick</span><span>Deadband + gentle shaping</span><span>Bounded roll/pitch and yaw-rate targets</span><span>Graphs only → later PID/mixer</span></div><p>The next firmware revision adds visible setpoints. It is not part of the measured run above and requires its own combined-input validation. Motors stay disabled; altitude control still requires an appropriate sensor. This is a finite software milestone, not another calibration campaign.</p>'''
core=core.replace('ROWS',str(len(q))).replace('NMIN',f'{norm.min():.9f}').replace('NMAX',f'{norm.max():.9f}').replace('STEP',f'{delta.max():.2f} ')
